  "query_preview": "..\nA low level inversion will trap low level moisture near the \nsurface again tonight with areas of low clouds and fog expected. \n\nFog aside, an upper-level ridge over the area will result in \nwarmer than average temperatures through Monday.\n\nThe ridge aloft will gradually move to the east Monday and Tuesday \nahead of an upper level trough that that will set over the \nPacific Northwest, resulting in SW flow aloft for Monday and\nTuesday. A shortwave trough ahead of the main upper trough will\nmove through eastern Montana Tuesday night and Wednesday with a\ncold front and light rain that will be mixed with snow at times. \n\nThe main upper trough settles into the Pacific Coast Wednesday and\nThursday and moves inland Friday and Saturday. Temperatures will\nbe closer to normal Wednesday and beyond across the region. \n\nFORECAST CONFIDENCE & DEVIATIONS:\n\n- Changes from NBM were to increase the areal coverage for fog \n  overnight and Sunday morning to most areas north of the Missouri\n  River and east of MT 13 and 253 south of the Missouri River. . \n \n- Confidence in fog occuring overnight and Sunday morning is high\n  but low in exactly where fog will develop. \n \n- Confidence in fair conditions persisting through Monday is high. \n\n- Confidence in mid week pattern change is high.\n\n- Confidence in precipitation Tuesday night and Wednesday is\n  moderate.",
